    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d653">

                <pb facs="1502" n="1486" sameAs="frus:frus1951v01/pg1486"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">394.31/10–351: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Canada</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Woodward</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ottawa</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-10-03T16:00:00-05:00">October 3, 1951—4
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 9—2:23 p. m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d653-f1"
                        />]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>57. From Willoughby. Embtel 53, Sept 28. Canadians agree generally with substance
                    Deptel Gatt 33 Sept 22. US position, however, apparently based on assumption
                    Belg restrictions not yet in effect. Canadians now certain licenses being
                    refused imports from Canada.</p>
                <p>Canadian Govt, therefore, instructing its del GATT today to take immediate steps
                    to seek removal existing restrictions. First Canadian approach will be informal
                    through Isbister<anchor n="2" xml:id="d653-f2"/> to head Belg del Geneva. If
                    ineffective, formal complaint to CPs planned.</p>
                <p>Belg case will set important precedent. While full info re activities EPU and
                    OEEC not available here, vigorous action objecting restrictions appears
                    imperative. Sterling area countries cld reasonably contend their monetary
                    arrangements analogous to EPU. If US acquiesces discriminatory import
                    restrictions under good current condition Belg reserves and balance payments, we
                    will have undermined basis for insisting elimination discriminatory restrictions
                    by sterling area countries as the balance payments improve.</p>
                <p>From standpoint Canada as well as other countries Belg precedent dangerous.
                    Canada has over-all current account deficit resulting chiefly from heavy trade
                    deficit with US. Serious balance payments difficulties <pb facs="1503" n="1487"
                        xml:id="pg1487"/> not impending but situation cld change rapidly. A large
                    reduction in capital imports which now offset current account deficit wld immed
                    confront Canada with problem of reducing trade deficit and strong pressure for
                    discriminatory restrictions against US trade wld develop.</p>
                <p>Dept pass Geneva, Paris. Sent Dept priority 57, rptd priority Geneva (for USDel
                    GATT) unn, Paris (for OSR) unn. [Willoughby.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Woodward</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d653-f1" n="1" xml:id="d653-f1-content">
                <p>Typewritten notation at the bottom of the page reads: “Message delayed in
                    transmission. Passed to Geneva, Paris, 10–9–51, 2:35 p. m.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d653-f2" n="2" xml:id="d653-f2-content">
                <p>C. M. Isbister of the Canadian Department of Trade and Commerce, member of the
                    Canadian Delegation at Geneva.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
